  playerTransform: {fileID: 0}
  screenOffset: {x: 0, y: 700}
  followSmoothness: 5
  shuffleFacts: 1
  loopFacts: 1
  facts:
  - Even touching or smelling certain foods can trigger allergic reactions for some
    people.
  - The most common food allergens include milk, eggs, peanuts, tree nuts, soy, wheat,
    fish, and shellfish.
  - Peanut allergies are one of the most common allergies among teens and can last
    a lifetime.
  - "Some allergic reactions don\u2019t happen right away\u2014they can appear minutes
    or even hours after eating."
  - Food labels are like secret clues! Reading them helps you avoid hidden allergens
    in snacks and meals.
  - "Lactose intolerance is not the same as a milk allergy\u2014one affects digestion,
    the other affects the immune system."
  - "Sharing food with friends is fun, but it can be risky if allergies aren\u2019t
    checked first."
  - "Cooking doesn\u2019t always remove allergens\u2014some proteins stay dangerous
    even after heating."
  - Knowing your allergens is a power-up skill that helps you stay safe and strong
    in real life and in NutriVenture!
  - "Allergic reactions can affect the skin, stomach, lungs, and heart\u2014not just
    one part of the body."
  - Hives, swelling, coughing, and dizziness can all be signs of a food allergy reaction.
  - Some sauces, candies, and drinks may contain hidden allergens like milk or nuts.
  - "Cross-contact happens when allergen foods touch safe foods\u2014making them
    unsafe too."
  - "Reading food labels every time matters\u2014recipes and ingredients can change!"
  - Teens with food allergies should always tell friends and teachers about their
    allergens.
  - "An allergic reaction can happen even if you\u2019ve eaten that food safely before."
  - Carrying emergency medicine is like having a shield against allergic reactions.
